<ol>
<li><strong>You could provide a child with a bit of unique nostalgia</strong></li>
</ol>
<p>As an adult, there are few things as satisfying as getting together with people of a similar age and playing, “Hey, remember [BLANK]?”</p>
<p>Maybe it’s something specific to you and whoever you’re playing it with. “Hey, remember Jake from down the hall in college who was always getting busted for having exotic pets in his dorm room?” But often, it’s something more general. “Hey, remember those old Mentos commercials?” Or else, it’s something that awakens your inner child, like a toy. “Hey, remember that game, <em>Don’t Break the Ice</em>?” Or “Hey, remember all the Tamagotchi knock-offs?”</p>
<p>But while it can be fun to bond, there’s something especially thrilling about stumping the other person. For example, hey, remember <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cybiko">the Cybiko</a>? You probably don’t. But I do. Because I had one. And let me tell you, it was an absolute piece of shit! But it was <em>my</em> piece of shit. And while I can relate to hundreds of others who played roughly twenty-eight seconds’ worth of Virtual Boy before pulling away to massage their eyes, never to return, it’s exciting to have this little bit of nostalgia that feels unique to me.</p>
<p>And now you can give your kids the same! Because, in twenty years, when they say, “Hey, remember ReactionWhizz?” nobody’s going to have any idea what the hell they’re talking about. And that will make them feel special.</p>
